//? Title: The Z-Algorithm: Reusing Prefix Matches to Guarantee Linear Work
//? Subtitle: A trace of the rightmost Z-box, binary-safe search and Titanic passenger-name queries
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
The Z-array records how much of a string's prefix reappears at every
position. Maintaining the rightmost known prefix-match interval lets the
algorithm reuse earlier answers, limiting explicit equality tests to
at most twice the input length. We derive that invariant, inspect an
actual trace, and reduce exact substring matching to a virtual sequence
with a separator outside the byte alphabet. The application searches
`dm::datasets::titanic()` passenger names independently and records both
matches and actual comparison work. A repetitive-string check contrasts
the linear algorithm with the quadratic work of naive prefix matching.
)

\toc

> The data: separate passenger-name strings

The Titanic frame contains 891 passenger records and 12 columns. We use
its `Name` column directly, preserving case and punctuation. Titles such
as `Miss.` and `Mrs.` provide frequent exact queries, while `William` and
`Anna` also illustrate matches within other parts of a name. These are
byte-substring searches, not a parser for titles or given names.

```{cpp, results="markdown", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  const auto passengers = dm::datasets::titanic();
  std::print("{}\n", passengers.select({"PassengerId", "Name"}).head(6).to_md());
  std::print("\n{}\n", dm::algorithms::z_search_summary(passengers, "Name", {"Miss."})
      .select({"records", "text_bytes", "matches", "matched_records"}).to_md());
}
```
