> The method: one prefix question at every position

For a sequence \(S\) of length \(n\), define

$$
Z[i]=\max\{k:0\le k\le n-i,\ S[0,k)=S[i,i+k)\}.
$$
\alttext(Z at position i is the largest length k for which the sequence prefix of length k equals the substring of length k starting at i.)

This implementation sets \(Z[0]=n\); some conventions leave it zero.
A naive algorithm compares the prefix separately against every suffix.
For \(S=a^n\), its comparisons total \(n(n-1)/2\), even though the
answers have an obvious repeated structure.

The Z-algorithm maintains a half-open interval \([\ell,r)\), the
rightmost prefix-match box discovered at positions already processed:
\(S[\ell,r)=S[0,r-\ell)\). The operational scan starts at position
one with an empty box, independently of the \(Z[0]=n\) convention.
At the next position \(i\), choose a starting match length

$$
b_i=\begin{cases}
0,& i\ge r,\\
\min\{r-i,Z[i-\ell]\},& i<r.
\end{cases}
$$
\alttext(The initial match length is zero outside the known box. Inside it, reuse the smaller of the remaining box length and the Z-value at the mirrored prefix position.)

The mirror index \(i-\ell\) is smaller than \(i\), so its answer is
already available. Capping at \(r-i\) prevents us from claiming anything
past the verified box. Starting from \(b_i\), compare symbols until a
mismatch or the sequence end, then replace the box by
\([i,i+Z[i])\) when that reaches farther right.

>> Why reuse is correct

If \(i<r\), the box identity copies the relevant region back into the
prefix. When \(Z[i-\ell]<r-i\), its terminating mismatch also lies
inside the box and transfers to the new position: the copied answer is
already exact. The shared library loop may perform that one known failing
test, but it cannot gain a successful extension there.

If \(Z[i-\ell]\ge r-i\), only the remaining box length is established;
further comparisons must start at the old right edge. Outside the box,
no characters can be reused. In every case, the seed is valid and direct
extension finds the exact answer.

>> Why the comparison count is linear

Every successful explicit comparison reaches beyond the old right edge;
no later position needs to verify that same extension of the frontier
again. There are at most \(n-1\) such successes. Each processed position
can terminate with at most one failing equality test, giving

$$
C_{\rm success}\le n-1,\qquad
C_{\rm fail}\le n-1,\qquad C\le2(n-1)\quad(n>0).
$$
\alttext(There are at most n minus one successful extension tests and n minus one failing tests, so total equality comparisons are at most twice n minus one.)

Bounds checks and loop bookkeeping add constant work per position.
Thus time and Z-array storage are \(O(n)\), even for highly repetitive
inputs. The counters below measure equality tests from the actual loop;
they are not estimates from another implementation.

> Following the rightmost box

The small self-similar sequence `aabaabaab`, retained from the old report,
contains nested prefix matches: \(Z[3]=6\) and \(Z[6]=3\). Its trace
shows both the copied seed and the final answer, making skipped work
visible rather than merely printing the final array.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/z_algorithm_frontier.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  const auto trace = dm::algorithms::z_array_trace("aabaabaab");
  std::print("{}\n", trace.select({"position", "z", "seed", "left_before", "right_before", "right_after", "comparisons"}).to_md());
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  dm::graphics::RPlot::create()
      .line(trace.get_column("position").values(), trace.get_column("right_after").values(),
            "verified endpoint r", dm::RGB{37, 99, 235}, 2)
      .points(trace.get_column("position").values(), trace.get_column("right_after").values(), "", dm::RGB{37, 99, 235}, 4)
      .title("The Z-box frontier advances and is then reused")
      .x_label("processed sequence position").y_label("right endpoint r (exclusive)")
      .y_limits(0, 10).size(900, 460).save_svg("build/z_algorithm_frontier.svg");
}
```
