| pattern | records | text_bytes | matches | matched_records | comparisons | processed_symbols | comparison_bound | box_reuses |
| :--- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| Mr. | 891 | 24026 | 517 | 517 | 28992 | 27590 | 53398 | 1167 |
| Mrs. | 891 | 24026 | 125 | 125 | 29620 | 28481 | 55180 | 904 |
| Miss. | 891 | 24026 | 182 | 182 | 30361 | 29372 | 56962 | 754 |
| Master. | 891 | 24026 | 40 | 40 | 31787 | 31154 | 60526 | 398 |
| William | 891 | 24026 | 70 | 69 | 30846 | 31154 | 60526 | 456 |
| Anna | 891 | 24026 | 17 | 17 | 27956 | 28481 | 55180 | 122 |

\image(figures/z_algorithm_names.svg)
// result_end
\caption(Occurrence counts, matched-record counts, actual comparisons, processed lengths and box reuses for six queries. The red markers are rigorous bounds, not performance targets.)
\alttext(Bars of observed equality comparisons for six name queries, all below their per-record summed linear-bound markers.)

The six query totals agree with an independent byte-string search.
`William` occurs 70 times across 69 records, showing why occurrences and
matched records are separate columns. All observed comparison totals lie
below their summed linear bounds; the maximum is still small on these
short names.

Searching six patterns independently repeats preprocessing six times.
For a large dictionary, an automaton such as Aho–Corasick can share work
across patterns. Conversely, the right-to-left skipping methods in the
Zhu–Takaoka report can use fewer comparisons on short names even without
Z's worst-case linear guarantee. Comparison counts alone do not include
allocation, cache behaviour or wall-clock time.

> A repetitive worst case for naive preprocessing

For \(a^n\), the first shifted prefix match extends through \(n-1\)
symbols. Every later match is copied to the end from the established box,
so the Z-algorithm makes exactly \(n-1\) equality tests. The naive method
repeats the already-known suffix matches:

$$
C_Z=n-1,\qquad C_{\rm naive}=\sum_{i=1}^{n-1}(n-i)=\frac{n(n-1)}2.
$$
\alttext(Z preprocessing of a repeated-byte string needs n minus one comparisons, whereas independent naive suffix comparisons sum to n times n minus one over two.)

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/z_algorithm_growth.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  std::vector<double> log_n, log_z, log_naive;
  std::print("| n | actual Z tests | Z bound | naive closed form | box reuses |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  for (std::size_t n : {16, 64, 256, 1024, 4096}) {
    const auto work = dm::algorithms::z_array_stats(std::string(n, 'a'));
    const auto naive = n * (n - 1) / 2;
    std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} | {} |\n", n, work.comparisons, 2 * (n-1), naive, work.box_reuses);
    log_n.push_back(std::log10(n)); log_z.push_back(std::log10(work.comparisons)); log_naive.push_back(std::log10(naive));
  }
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  dm::graphics::RPlot::create()
      .line(log_n, log_z, "Z: measured", dm::RGB{37, 99, 235}, 2)
      .line(log_n, log_naive, "naive: formula", dm::RGB{220, 38, 38}, 2)
      .title("Repeated bytes: linear reuse versus quadratic rechecking")
      .x_label("log10(sequence length)").y_label("log10(equality tests)")
      .size(900, 460).save_svg("build/z_algorithm_growth.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| n | actual Z tests | Z bound | naive closed form | box reuses |
| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| 16 | 15 | 30 | 120 | 14 |
| 64 | 63 | 126 | 2016 | 62 |
| 256 | 255 | 510 | 32640 | 254 |
| 1024 | 1023 | 2046 | 523776 | 1022 |
| 4096 | 4095 | 8190 | 8386560 | 4094 |
\image(figures/z_algorithm_growth.svg)
// result_end
\caption(The actual Z counts agree with n minus one at every tested sequence length. The naive curve is the derived closed form; it is not a timing measurement.)
\alttext(A log-log plot where Z comparisons have slope near one and naive comparisons have slope near two.)

> Conclusion

The Z-box makes verified equality reusable: a copied seed is capped by the
known interval, and any new successful tests advance its frontier.
Instrumentation connects that invariant to the linear comparison bound.
The same preprocessing gives exact byte-substring search with overlaps
and no reserved input symbol. Passenger-name queries provide a real
record-wise application; repeated-byte strings verify the case where
naive preprocessing wastes the most already-known work.

> References

- D. Gusfield (1997). *Algorithms on Strings, Trees, and Sequences*, chapter 1,
  fundamental preprocessing. Cambridge University Press. https://doi.org/10.1017/CBO9780511574931
